WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:05.620
I’m looking at a map of surface temperatures
in the Phoenix metro area.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:10.545
So this is in Arizona, in the southwest US
and it shows how hot the ground is in June.

00:00:10.545 --> 00:00:13.410
And this is a map of child poverty rates.

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:15.619
Darker blue means more poverty.

00:00:15.619 --> 00:00:16.619
So this is heat.

00:00:16.619 --> 00:00:18.369
And this is poverty.

00:00:18.369 --> 00:00:19.369
Heat.

00:00:19.369 --> 00:00:20.369
Poverty.

00:00:20.369 --> 00:00:21.570
Heat, Poverty.&nbsp;

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.470
The correlation is pretty obvious.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:28.609
This is where it becomes clear that climate
change and wealth inequality are not really

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:29.609
separate issues.

00:00:29.609 --> 00:00:34.070
And you can probably find examples of that
wherever you go in the world, but for this

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:36.560
story, I went... there.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.320
"An excessive heat warning is in effect."

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.670
"Warmer is the new normal in Phoenix."

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.570
"The city's grappling with an increase in
heat related deaths."

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:49.550
"Phoenix, last year, hitting 100 degrees or
higher for more than 144 days."

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:53.054
"Climate change is one factor causing rising
temperature.

00:00:53.054 --> 00:00:56.168
"Urban development is the other."

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:02.030
The forecasted high that day was 112 degrees
Fahrenheit.

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:04.750
That's 44 degrees Celsius.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:09.070
But in reality, some areas can feel warmer
or cooler than that.

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:13.021
So here I am in a neighborhood with some of
the hottest surface temperatures, but when

00:01:13.021 --> 00:01:18.430
I drove 8 miles north of there, I was in one
of the coolest parts of Phoenix.

00:01:19.861 --> 00:01:23.549
And it was in the hot neighborhood where I
met up with Eva Olivas.

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:26.310
She's a local community advocate and Phoenix
native.

00:01:26.651 --> 00:01:27.386
It's nice to meet you.

00:01:27.386 --> 00:01:28.339
OLIVAS: It's nice to meet you.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.809
Can you tell me a little bit about this
neighborhood and who lives here?

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:38.279
OLIVAS: So the community of Central City South
is located just south of the downtown business district.

00:01:38.279 --> 00:01:42.650
OLIVAS: The average income level for a family of four
is about $19,000.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.490
OLIVAS: The vehicle ownership rate is between 23-28%
of the population.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:46.490
Oh wow.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:47.490
OLIVAS:  Yes, very low.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:50.525
So they’re very dependent on public transportation.

00:01:50.525 --> 00:01:55.270
This zip code has the highest rate of
heat-related illnesses in the city, what makes

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:58.008
people here vulnerable to extreme heat?

00:01:58.008 --> 00:02:00.509
Well, I think it’s a combination of
things.

00:02:00.509 --> 00:02:02.420
We have the freeway.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:04.649
OLIVAS: We have the railroad tracks on the north.

00:02:04.649 --> 00:02:06.140
OLIVAS: We have the airport on the east.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:07.494
OLIVAS: So we're like a little pocket.

00:02:07.494 --> 00:02:08.650
That's a lot of pavement.

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:13.380
OLIVAS: A lot of concrete, a lot of industry
here.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.160
A lot of our streets, as you can see, don't
have sidewalks.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.632
You're having to walk in the street, like
we are.

00:02:20.632 --> 00:02:22.250
And there's not a lot of shade.

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:26.380
If you can look around, you don't see a lot
of trees.

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:29.390
This is the Sonoran Desert, so of course the
summers are hot.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:34.290
But it also seems like this entire city was
almost designed to capture heat.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.150
The streets are so wide.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.650
This isn’t a highway, this is just the Phoenix
grid.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:43.240
And the buildings are so flat and set back
from the sidewalk, so they don’t create

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:44.850
much shade at all.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.210
And then there’s the parking lots.

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:52.190
I read one estimate that there are more than
4 parking spots for every car in Phoenix.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.020
The sun just beams into all that pavement.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:58.340
And it doesn't just roast anyone who happens
to walk across it, it’s also transferring

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:00.608
heat to the air, day and night.

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:05.296
Wow I really … did that poorly.

00:03:05.296 --> 00:03:09.000
Also, I promise this is not a Chevy ad, I
just borrowed my dad's car.

00:03:09.841 --> 00:03:13.300
So what do you do to try to mitigate heat
in a city like this?

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.690
Well, reflective roofs can help a bit.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:20.216
The thermal image shows the surface temperature
of the dark roof is hotter than the white ones.

00:03:20.216 --> 00:03:24.684
And the city has also tried painting some
roads with a more reflective coating.

00:03:25.351 --> 00:03:28.810
But if you compare the hottest and coolest
neighborhoods, it’s pretty clear that the

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:32.350
big difference between them isn’t the black
or white surfaces.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.874
It’s the green ones.

00:03:36.207 --> 00:03:41.660
Plants are the variable that connects this
map of heat to this map of poverty.

00:03:41.660 --> 00:03:45.376
And studies have confirmed that the cooling
effect of cool roofs and cool pavements is

00:03:45.376 --> 00:03:48.560
smaller than the cooling effect of trees.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.810
"One of the best ways to beat the urban heat."

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:53.430
"The more trees you plant, the more benefits."

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:54.520
"It's cleansing for the air."

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:55.700
"Reduce carbon footprint."

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:56.880
"Lower levels of stress."

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.290
"Decrease energy costs."

00:03:58.290 --> 00:03:59.510
"Storm water management."

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:01.779
"It's a very important engine of cooling."

00:04:01.779 --> 00:04:05.760
If you take an area that’s just parking
lots and buildings, and cover at least a quarter

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.280
of the space with trees, you can lower air
temperatures there by around 8 degrees Fahrenheit

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:12.262
or 4.4 degrees Celsius.

00:04:12.262 --> 00:04:15.810
That’s according to a study from 
Arizona State University.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:19.930
For those who are used to humid summers, it
can be hard to understand just how much relief

00:04:19.930 --> 00:04:22.340
shade provides in a dry heat.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:26.123
You can easily see the temperature difference
on my arms and on the concrete there.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.720
So the city of Phoenix has committed funds
to planting more trees.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:35.251
They want all neighborhoods to reach a minimal
level of canopy cover by 2030.

00:04:36.145 --> 00:04:42.000
And I love the idea of recruiting an army of trees
to protect the vulnerable from extreme heat.

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:43.986
But there’s a problem.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:49.620
Outside of parks and along major streets,
the urban forest currently depends not on

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:53.220
public resources, but on private ones.

00:04:55.675 --> 00:05:00.470
What would it take for a street like this
one to become one of those shady tree-lined

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:02.506
streets that they have uptown.

00:05:02.506 --> 00:05:07.670
OLIVAS: Well, any tree that’s planted in this
neighborhood in front of someone’s home,

00:05:07.670 --> 00:05:09.330
they would have to maintain.

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:13.230
OLIVAS: The city does not maintain them 
in these residential areas.

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:14.710
I see, okay.

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:18.470
So there are some utility companies here that
will give you a couple of free trees if you

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:22.930
agree to plant them on the west side or south
side of your home, because eventually that

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:26.540
can end up saving something like 30% on your
air conditioning bill.

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:29.690
But it sounds like that doesn’t really solve
the maintenance issue.

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:30.690
OLIVAS: Correct.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:35.960
OLIVAS: Because there’s that tradeoff between shade
or increased water bill.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:39.810
But it’s the cost of maintaining the trees
that is challenging, obviously in a very low

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:41.420
income neighborhood like ours.

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:45.380
And it’s not a priority for them in their
family, in their home.

00:05:45.884 --> 00:05:50.230
Are there no programs here that are
actually going to take on the watering cost?

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:51.230
OLIVAS: Zero.

00:05:51.230 --> 00:05:54.960
That is the responsibility of the homeowner
and you have to remember, what did I say?

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:56.872
These are mostly renters.

00:05:57.555 --> 00:06:02.094
I walked with Eva along Buckeye Road where
there’s a big triangular lot, it’s owned

00:06:02.094 --> 00:06:04.390
by the city, and has been left unplanted.

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:07.840
She said that would probably change with the
city’s new tree equity plans.

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:12.900
But she also hoped for some more basic things:
like sidewalks, and shade shelters for all

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.180
the bus stops.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:19.450
It was a reminder that wealth already affects
access to infrastructure, even infrastructure

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:22.310
that doesn’t have to be watered and pruned.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:26.480
And that's what will have to change if cities
like Phoenix want to make sure that the heaviest

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:31.830
burdens of climate warming don’t fall on those
who are least prepared to withstand them.

